[TITLE]
L-Town: PODDS discolouration (turbidity) model - cohesive layer mobilisation and regeneration (Boxall et al. 2001; Boxall & Saul 2005)

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PODDS (Prediction Of Discolouration in Distribution Systems, University of
; Sheffield) written as an EPANET-MSX reaction file for L-Town (ltown-24h.inp).
; Our own implementation of the published equations; see models/literature.md.
;
; Sources (equation numbers as in Furnass 2015):
;  Boxall JB, Skipworth PJ, Saul AJ (2001) A novel approach to modelling
;    sediment movement in distribution mains based on particle characteristics.
;    Water Software Systems, Vol 1. Research Studies Press. (PODDS concept)
;  Boxall JB, Saul AJ (2005) Modeling discoloration in potable water
;    distribution systems. J Environ Eng 131(5):716-725.
;    doi:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9372(2005)131:5(716)
;  Husband PS, Boxall JB (2010) Field studies of discoloration in water
;    distribution systems: model verification and practical implications.
;    J Environ Eng 136(1):86-94. doi:10.1061/(ASCE)EE.1943-7870.0000115
;  Husband S, Boxall J (2015) Predictive water quality modelling and
;    resilience flow conditioning to manage discolouration risk in
;    operational trunk mains. J Water Supply Res T - Aqua 64(5):529-542.
;    doi:10.2166/aqua.2014.042 (Fig. 1: R = P(tau_a - tau)^n,
;    C = k*tau + Cmax, dC = R dt, dN = R As/Q). White Rose eprint 90591.
;  Furnass WR (2015) Modelling both the continual accumulation and erosion of
;    discolouration material in drinking water distribution systems. PhD
;    thesis, University of Sheffield (eqs 2.15-2.18; Table 5.6 parameter sets;
;    Figs 4.6, 5.9 regeneration). etheses.whiterose.ac.uk/10645
;  Husband S, Boxall JB (2016) Understanding and managing discolouration risk
;    in trunk mains. Water Res 107:127-140. doi:10.1016/j.watres.2016.10.049
;
; State: per pipe (MSX wall species, fixed in space along the pipe)
;   CPOT  discolouration potential C, TPMU/m2 = NTU.m (1 TPMU = 1 NTU.m3)
;   TCOND layer shear strength tau_c = (C - Cmax)/k        (eq 2.15, b = 1)
; Erosion when the applied shear tau_a exceeds tau_c:
;   R = P (tau_a - tau_c)^n                                (eq 2.16)
;   dC/dt = -R ; turbidity gain dN = R As/Q                 (eqs 2.18, 2.17)
;   In MSX's Lagrangian frame dN/dt = R * (wall area / water volume) = R*4/D.
; Regeneration (original PODDS coding: reverse of mobilisation, strongest
; layers first, linear in time) while tau_a < tau_c:   dC/dt = P0
;   This is the reduced form of the Boxall & Saul (2005) regeneration law with
;   its temperature and strength exponents set to zero (Furnass 2015, Fig 5.9).
; Applied shear: tau_a = rho g (D/4) S0 = rho f U^2 / 8 = rho u*^2, from MSX's
;   friction factor Ff (from the hydraulic headloss) and velocity U.
;   NOTE: the MSX hydraulic variable "Us" cannot be used in this MSX version:
;   the name lookup matches the prefix "U" first, so Us evaluates to U.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[OPTIONS]
  AREA_UNITS   M2          ;wall species per m2 of pipe wall
  RATE_UNITS   SEC         ;PODDS rates are per second
  SOLVER       RK5
  TIMESTEP     60          ;L-Town has many short pipes; 300 s doubles the turbidity error
  RTOL         0.001
  ATOL         0.001

[SPECIES]
  BULK  TURB   NTU    0.001  0.001   ;turbidity, treated as a concentration
  WALL  CPOT   TPMU   0.001  0.001   ;PODDS discolouration potential C, TPMU per m2 of wall
  WALL  TCOND  PA                    ;layer shear strength tau_c (formula, reported)
  WALL  TAPP   PA                    ;applied wall shear stress tau_a (formula, reported)

[COEFFICIENTS]
  CONSTANT  LU  1.0     ;metres per network length unit: 1 for SI networks, 0.3048 for US units
; PODDS mobilisation parameters: set "PODDS-2" (100 mm pipe) of Furnass (2015)
; Table 5.6. The calibrated PODDS look-up table is confidential (not published).
  PARAMETER  K     -0.5      ;k, TPMU/m2 per Pa (negative: C falls as tau_c rises)
  PARAMETER  P      0.0002   ;P, TPMU/m2 per Pa^n per s (erosion coefficient)
  PARAMETER  N      2.5      ;n, - (erosion exponent)
; Cmax sets the ultimate layer strength -Cmax/k = 1.8 Pa (strongest material in
; plastic pipes, Husband & Boxall 2010, as reported by Furnass 2015 2.2.8.4)
  PARAMETER  CMAX   0.9      ;Cmax, TPMU/m2
; Regeneration: full layer (C from 0 to Cmax) in about 1.75 years
; (Husband & Boxall 2015 Aqua: ~2 years; Husband, Boxall & Sunny 2023: 1.65-1.85 y)
  PARAMETER  P0     1.6E-8   ;P0, TPMU/m2 per s
  CONSTANT   RHO    1000     ;water density, kg/m3
  CONSTANT   LPM3   1000     ;litres per m3 (MSX bulk concentrations are per litre)

[TERMS]
  ;lengths and velocities in metres: LU is metres per network length unit
  ULU     U*LU
  TAUA   RHO*ULU^2*Ff/8        ;applied wall shear stress tau_a, Pa (U in m/s)
  TAUC   (CPOT-CMAX)/K       ;layer shear strength tau_c, Pa (eq 2.15)
  XS     TAUA-TAUC           ;excess shear stress, Pa
  XSP    XS*STEP(XS)         ;excess shear when eroding, else 0
  EROS   P*XSP^N*STEP(CPOT)  ;R, TPMU/m2/s: supply rate from 1 m2 of wall (eq 2.16)
  REGEN  P0*STEP(-XS)        ;regeneration while tau_a < tau_c

[PIPES]
  RATE     CPOT   REGEN-EROS          ;eq 2.18 plus regeneration
  RATE     TURB   LPM3*Av*EROS        ;eq 2.17: Av is m2 of wall per litre, NTU = TPMU/m3
  FORMULA  TCOND  TAUC
  FORMULA  TAPP   TAUA

[TANKS]
  RATE     TURB   0                   ;mobilised material stays entrained

[QUALITY]
; Background turbidity of the treated water at both reservoirs and everywhere
  GLOBAL  TURB  0.1
  NODE    R1    TURB  0.1
  NODE    R2    TURB  0.1
; Initial layer condition. PODDS assumes layers in equilibrium with a
; conditioning shear: C = Cmax + k*tau_cond. Here each pipe's layers were
; conditioned at half of the peak shear it sees in this 24 h run (peak flows
; ~45% above the conditioning flows, e.g. a hot-weather day), with a floor of
; 0.02 Pa: GLOBAL covers pipes whose half-peak is below 0.02 Pa, the LINK
; lines the rest (peak shear from rho*U^2*Ff/8 at the 5 min hydraulic steps).
  GLOBAL  CPOT  0.89
  LINK  p1     CPOT  0.88110
  LINK  p2     CPOT  0.88568
  LINK  p3     CPOT  0.86032
  LINK  p9     CPOT  0.86105
